---
title: Client tiers
summary: Why evaluating a model and adding density terms to it are separate traits, ModelClientOps and ModelBuilderOps, so an estimator can be given one without the other.
order: 9
---

## Context

An estimator needs to evaluate and differentiate a model. It should not be able to add factors
to it or tear it down.

## Decision

The client surface is split into two traits. `ModelClientOps` evaluates, differentiates and reads
the model. It can also add constants and parameters, `observe`, `demote_to_parameter`, and remove
leaves. `ModelBuilderOps: ModelClientOps` adds density terms (`add_quantity`, `add_proposal`,
`add_factor`), `stacked_tangent`, and `remove_closure`.

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-model/src/client_ops.rs:300..307}
```

| | Tier | Behaviour |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `remove` | `ModelClientOps` | Leaf-only, all-or-nothing. Refuses a node that is still referenced or still bound. |
| `remove_closure` | `ModelBuilderOps` | Takes the downward closure and its referrers. Cannot fail on a live child. |

## Consequences

- An estimator holding only `ModelClientOps` cannot silently delete a factor it was handed.
- The boundary is pinned by a compile-fail test
  (`crates/skoro-model/tests/compile_fail/remove_closure_is_not_a_client_op.rs`).
- Neither trait says anything about what a model *contains*; see
  [Arity is the capability test](../no-capability-flags/).
